Exercise 4.15 asks to show that a monotone endomap \(f\) of the elements of a domain has a maximal fixed point, meaning a fixed point that cannot be extended to a strictly larger fixed point, by applying Zorn’s lemma to a maximal chain in \(\{x \mid x \sqsubseteq f(x)\}\) and using 2.11 to obtain the supremum of that chain, and then to deduce a least fixed point. `chainUnion_le` says the union of a nonempty chain of elements lies under every upper bound of the chain, because a neighbourhood of the union belongs to some member and that member lies under the upper bound. `exists_maximal_fixedPoint` applies Zorn’s lemma to the set of post-fixed points: the empty chain is bounded by bottom, which is post-fixed because bottom lies under its image, and a nonempty chain is bounded by its union, which is post-fixed because a neighbourhood of the union belongs to a member, that member lies under the image of the union by monotonicity, and the member is post-fixed. A maximal post-fixed point lies under its image, the image is post-fixed, and maximality forces the image back under the point, so the two agree; any fixed point above it is post-fixed and hence below it. `exists_least_fixedPoint` takes such a maximal fixed point as a witness that some element \(a\) satisfies \(f(a) \sqsubseteq a\), and applies `monoFix`, the intersection of all pre-fixed points from Exercise 4.13, which is a fixed point lying under every fixed point. `example_415` is the conjunction of the least-fixed-point statement and the maximal-fixed-point statement. **Theorem:** yes — a monotone endomap has both a least fixed point and a maximal fixed point in the sense that any larger fixed point is equal to it. **Proof:** yes — Zorn on the post-fixed points, with chain unions as bounds, is the upper-bound form of the hinted maximal-chain argument, and the least fixed point is the Exercise 4.13 intersection once a pre-fixed point is known to exist.
